/** worker-slot-3 — siman 551 bad_mt hand translations (67 keys) */
export const HAND = {
  "baer-heitev/2:א":
    "Joy. This refers also to commerce such as wedding needs; but other commerce is permitted as usual — Beit Yosef, Bach; Maharil wrote: brokers should not carry silver vessels to the broker in the city, for joy is for onlookers. Where they practice prohibition in all commerce, release is needed — see Yoreh De'ah siman 214; but where they practice reducing all commerce, release does not help since some poskim already hold so as that view. Siman 554 s.k. 22 implies reduction in all commerce — see there; Beit Yosef in name of Ran: any building not needed except mere profit is forbidden; if he contracted with a gentile to paint his house — permitted to paint; if he can appease him with little to wait until after Tisha B'Av — excellent, Maharil; it appears permitted, for it is a rabbinic mitzvah. Magen Avraham:",
  "baer-heitev/5:_":
    "The gentile. In private one may be lenient; likewise where gentiles have distinct clothing recognizable as theirs — permitted as Or Sameach in gloss. Magen Avraham:",
  "baer-heitev/7:ב":
    "From Rosh Chodesh. Regarding wearing: Mechaber and Beit Yosef are lenient because the reason for prohibiting work as seif 8 — they practiced not to spin wool threads from fear it was decreed on Tisha B'Av the cornerstone, and all garment repair is in this category since spinning is the start of melacha — Beit Yosef; Darkei Moshe wrote from this reason even making for others to sell in the market is forbidden; Tashbetz implies even making to sell is forbidden; Magen Avraham wrote it appears permitted to make what they call \"kronin\" woven on wood, for it is not a garment:",
  "baer-heitev/8:_":
    "After. Siman 426 seif 2: the Ari z\"l wrote one may sanctify the moon on motza'ei Tisha B'Av, for on motza'ei Tisha B'Av the son of David was born and they announce to the moon and Israel they will be renewed:",
  "baer-heitev/16:א":
    "From Rosh Chodesh. It appears a woman giving birth may bathe as Yoreh De'ah 481; Berachot 15b — Tosafot wrote even on Tisha B'Av she may bathe — see there; do not be lenient, for we are not so accustomed to bathing. Rosh Chodesh on erev Shabbat — some wash for Shabbat honor, some Thursday for clothing; customs. Bach wrote: one accustomed to wash every erev Shabbat who refrains now commits a transgression; sufficient if stringent on Shabbat Chazon. Knesset HaGedolah: many daughters laundered on Tisha B'Av after chatzot — I overlooked them; if they ask, forbid; on this Shulchan Aruch wrote women practice washing hair from Mincha upward; early ones instituted this because the anointed one was born on Tisha B'Av and a reminder of the redeemer is needed — specifically women who do not know scripture to believe in consolation. If Tisha B'Av is Thursday, forbidden to launder Thursday for Shabbat honor even from Mincha — Ran, Beit Yosef, Darkei Moshe, Levush; Bach: Thursday of Tisha B'Av after chatzot permitted to launder for Shabbat when impossible to wait until erev Shabbat because of Shabbat honor; likewise one traveling far this Shabbat may launder linen garments for several Shabboses — certainly impossible to wait until after Tisha B'Av; nevertheless better through a gentile — end quote; Magen Avraham s.k. 18 disagrees with Bach, wrote no permission at all Thursday, all the more for a traveler forbidden to launder for Shabbat need after erev Shabbat; see Taz s.k. 14:",
  "baer-heitev/17:א":
    "Shehecheyanu. On intervening Shabboses permitted to bless Shehecheyanu — Mateh Moshe, Levush. In writings of the Ari — forbidden even on Shabbat; it appears if 17 Tammuz falls on Shabbat and is postponed, permitted for everyone. Magen Avraham:",
  "beer-hagolah/2:א": "Baraita and Gemara Yevamot 42, and Ramban's explanation siman 405, and Tur brought it",
  "beer-hagolah/2:ח": "Tur in name of Rabbenu Nissim from Yerushalmi",
  "beer-hagolah/3:ה": "Tur in name of Avi HaEzri",
  "beer-hagolah/4:ג": "Tur in name of Semag and Semak",
  "beer-hagolah/6:_": "Tur in name of Ramban",
  "beer-hagolah/7:ב": "Tur per his view",
  "beer-hagolah/12:_": "Tur in name of Ramban",
  "beer-hagolah/16:א": "Tur in name of Avi HaEzri",
  "beer-hagolah/16:ג": "Tur from the day the city was breached; some say corresponding to the three weeks Daniel fasted:",
  "beer-hagolah/18:_": "Hagahot Maimoniyot Chadashot and Rokeach siman 90 from Midrash Eichah Rabbati and Midrash Tehillim on Psalm 91.",
  "machatzit-hashekel/16:א": "(s.k. 16) After, etc. — no release needed, R' Shlomo responsum 54:",
  "machatzit-hashekel/16:ד":
    "Further it appears since Rashbag holds, etc. — they practice like Rashbag as it should be. Magen Avraham's intent here is not to answer Maharshal's question; on the contrary this is Maharshal's question — how may he rule like Rashbag against Rava's ruling; Magen Avraham too holds Bach's answer; only it appears from Bach's words it is mere stringency; if so if 15 Av is before Shabbat they may eat meat that day, since it is among days without tachanun as above siman 531; therefore Magen Avraham wrote: presumably the stringent, although they concede by law permitted after the fast as Rava like R' Meir, nevertheless add stringency like Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel as Bach; since they are stringent like Rashbag, and for Rashbag forbidden by law, certainly for them forbidden even on 15 Av; if so they are stringent like Rashbag also for them forbidden on 15; this is from the anonymous wording:",
  "machatzit-hashekel/18:ה":
    "For the launderers of the house of Rav were idle — in Taanit daf 29 he proved R' Shimon: even to launder and leave forbidden on Shabbat when Tisha B'Av falls therein — from the launderers of Rav's house being idle that Shabbat; if so still a question: certainly in Rav's great academy people traveled far and it would be forbidden for them to launder — if so this too is forbidden; Taz s.k. 14; see Or Sameach:",
  "magen-avraham/2:ד":
    "Lest another precede him. And why permitted to make betrothal feast? Nevertheless it appears forbidden to make a feast even without dancing; some permit for milah feast since no joy at all unlike here; it appears forbidden even on Shabbat, for the Gemara teaches from Rosh Chodesh until the fast forbidden — impossible without Shabbat; nevertheless what they practice eating sweets when writing conditions is not called a feast; it appears forbidden to make dancing from 17 Tammuz onward:",
  "magen-avraham/3:ד":
    "And likewise for Shabbat honor. Implies even for Shabbat honor permitted to launder on Thursday; so Tur and Mishnah; so appears simple; so Bach this siman they practice permission; Darkei Moshe wrote: we should have permitted bathing on erev Shabbat from a fortiori laundering forbidden by law yet permitted for Shabbat honor — all the more bathing; nevertheless they practiced prohibition even in bathing — end quote; implies in laundering too they practiced prohibition; nevertheless lenient if he has no undershirt for Shabbat; so Hagahot Maimoniyot; through gentile obviously permitted; Hagahot Ashiri wrote haircut forbidden even for Shabbat honor; it appears the reason: otherwise they are not accustomed to cut hair every week:",
  "magen-avraham/6:א":
    "New garments. It appears customary to press garments in a press — then they are giyutz and forbidden by law; why they practiced prohibition in Shabbat garments: although worn many Shabboses, their giyutz is still evident:",
  "magen-avraham/9:ג":
    "And they hide knives. Yoreh De'ah siman 217: permitted to pierce animals; Mishna Berurah: no slaughter until 10 Av; if Tisha B'Av is Thursday, permitted to slaughter after chatzot for Shabbat honor [Bach]; in Avodaraham wrote the reason from Rosh Chodesh until fast they decreed lest one come to eat from it; on the fast day itself no decree applies; if so always permitted; siman 559 seif 6; it appears in our country gentiles do not eat meat on Friday and Shabbat — therefore no buyers for raw meat; permitted to slaughter Thursday lest they refrain and not slaughter; so siman 497 s.k. 3; siman 554 s.k. 23; one who cannot eat dairy may eat poultry meat not offered as sacrifice and no joy except in animal meat (Bach):",
  "magen-avraham/16:א":
    "Not to bathe. It appears a woman giving birth may bathe as Yoreh De'ah 481:3; Berachot 16b — Tosafot wrote even on Tisha B'Av permitted to bathe — see there; do not be lenient, for we are not so accustomed to bathing; Rosh Chodesh on erev Shabbat some wash for Shabbat, some Thursday (Levush, customs); Bach: one accustomed to wash every erev Shabbat who refrains commits transgression; sufficient if stringent on Shabbat Chazon:",
  "mishnah-berurah/2:א":
    "(11) We reduce commerce — some poskim hold this means not to engage in joyful commerce such as buying silver vessels or wedding needs and the like; but ordinary commerce need not be reduced; some hold all commerce must be reduced now and one should engage only for livelihood; so siman 554 end s.k. 22 requires reducing all commerce; [only if it is a fair, perhaps it is great loss and permitted for everyone if found cheaper then]; in our time they are lenient in all this since everything is considered livelihood:",
  "mishnah-berurah/3:ס":
    "(34) To launder from Rosh Chodesh onward — even if told to launder after Tisha B'Av, forbidden because of custom [Magen Avraham]; Sefer Or Sameach sided to lenient to give from Rosh Chodesh to launder after Tisha B'Av; Pri Megadim brought it; regarding linen garments certainly rely on this:",
  "mishnah-berurah/9:ה":
    "(60) And they hide from Rosh Chodesh, etc. — until 10 Av; in pressing need lenient to slaughter on Tisha B'Av after chatzot; if Tisha B'Av is Thursday, all permit slaughter after chatzot for Shabbat honor; Magen Avraham and Or Sameach wrote: in our country gentile custom not to eat meat on Friday and Shabbat, perhaps meat will spoil and no buyers for raw meat — permitted to slaughter Thursday too [lest they refrain and not slaughter] so if spoiled he has it ready for Friday sale:",
  "mishnah-berurah/9:ו":
    "(61) For the ill — even slight illness; although siman 554 s.k. 9 Magen Avraham wrote some women practice avoiding meat and wine from 7 Av — that is without illness; one who rented from the ruler to feed meat to uncircumcised — if he does not slaughter he loses — give from each animal to a poor ill person some piece; impossible a kezayit meat without slaughter [Chayei Adam]:",
  "mishnah-berurah/15:ג":
    "(87) But not with combs — specifically when the thirtieth day falls in the week Tisha B'Av falls; when it falls earlier, permitted to cut hair and launder even for us who practice general prohibition here permitted since his mourning was compounded; so Taz in name of Bach; Ateret Zekenim holds not to be lenient except when the thirtieth falls before Rosh Chodesh, as many later authorities:",
  "mishnah-berurah/16:ד": "(91) Until Tisha B'Av — see below siman 562 seif 2 in gloss:",
  "turei-zahav/7:_":
    "Lest they spin wool. Meaning lest they make wool threads; Beit Yosef wrote: it appears not for him or others, whether for wages or free; the reason: fear of decree on Tisha B'Av the cornerstone was shattered — this applies in all cases:",
  "turei-zahav/17:_":
    "And likewise fruit he will not find, etc. It appears from this reason one may permit all days between the straits lest a person die before fulfilling this mitzvah; I heard this reasoning in name of Rav Yosef Katz of Krakow; there is support from what Tosafot brought chapter Eizehu Mekoman Beit Yosef siman 549: permitted l'chatchila anything related to court on chol hamoed, for it is great loss lest the giver or witnesses or court die, etc.; if for bodily needs they were lenient with this reasoning for needs of Heaven to bless on a Torah scroll lest he hurry to fulfill — and in Sefer Chasidim siman 843 permitted Shehecheyanu on Shabbat. Reason for avoiding meat and wine from 17 Tammuz until 9 Av that individuals and some fast completely has three reasons: first Daniel fasted this measure; second from 17 when the city was breached until 9 when the Temple was destroyed one grief; third Temple sacrifice and wine libation ceased 17 Tammuz; R' Shlomo responsum 92 wrote for all three bathing prohibition does not apply; so below bathing on erev Shabbat and Rosh Chodesh in name of Mateh Moshe; per these reasons one should be stringent even on Shabboses between the straits, except Shabbat honor they did not be stringent; it appears the stringent should not say they fear Rashbag's view — Heaven forbid disagreeing with Rava — rather be stringent beyond the letter for themselves silently. One who vows meat and wine these three weeks — even without stipulation for Shabbat permission — Shabbat is not included, general rule Yoreh De'ah 218: in vows we follow the neder's intent; here he intended to be among ascetics practicing this between the straits; on Shabbat they do not practice this stringency — certainly the neder's intent likewise; only vows to strengthen himself lest he regret if he does not vow; although I wrote Yoreh De'ah 220: \"I will not eat meat three weeks\" includes Shabboses — unlike here he specified these between the straits, certainly intent as practiced between the straits for destruction as above, not applicable on Shabbat where no between-the-straits stringency; Mateh Moshe wrote: even one practicing general prohibition between the straits does not practice on Shabbat; one who vows explicitly — Shabbat included; see responsum Chacham Tzvi 116 to distinguish short and long time; Ya'arot Devash third derashah part 2 until Shabbat is short — see there; since on Shabbat more reason to bless than weekday, they permitted Shabbat and forbade weekday — correct, since until Shabbat no death concern and he will bless then; see Magen Avraham 568 s.k. 13 and Machatzit HaShekel there. [Gaon responsum Orach Chayim 103]:",
  "beur-hagra/1:ב":
    "(1) But otherwise, etc. — not asked regarding Shabbat when Tisha B'Av falls within — forbidden by law, for even pre-laundered forbidden, all the more in the nine days; thirty permits laundering as chapter Chalitzah and first chapter Moed Katan; forbidden in laundering all seven; thirty is only giyutz; forbidden for Tanna Kamma and Rabbi in the nine days as third chapter Moed Katan; all the more where laundering forbidden, not even from Rosh Chodesh since they practiced laundering prohibition as seif 3 gloss:",
  "beur-hagra/3:נ":
    "(3) But on Tisha B'Av, etc. — as written they censure Abaye, etc.; what they said \"our Yom Tov,\" etc. — specifically Tisha B'Av postponed; see above siman 559:",
  "beur-hagra/4:ד":
    "(4) Except day, etc. — as written; on Thursday permitted because of Shabbat honor; likewise erev Shabbat as written; if he did not launder, etc.; although Abaye is censured when Tisha B'Av is erev Shabbat, here even l'chatchila permitted; that which they said Thursday erev Shabbat — when impossible otherwise, forbidden; even for Tanna of baraitot l'chatchila forbidden:",
  "beur-hagra/5:_":
    "(5) Forbidden, etc. — Yerushalmi there: a launderer is forbidden to work; implies forbidden for everyone, even a gentile. Beit Yosef:",
  "beur-hagra/10:ג":
    "(10) And permitted, etc. — for in such a case they did not accept as Hagahot Maimoniyot chapter 1 Hilchot Taanit regarding fast when milah occurred erev Shabbat:",
  "beur-hagra/16:א":
    "(16) Some practice, etc. — like a mourner; they forbade haircutting and laundering; all from kabbalah; but by law even on 9 Av permitted as end of Taanit: whatever is for, etc.:",
  "beur-hagra/16:ג":
    "(16) For need, etc. — for in such a case they did not accept upon themselves; even where forbidden by law they said Thursday permitted because of Shabbat honor; further bathing not for pleasure but for mitzvah permitted even on Tisha B'Av as written all obligated in immersions, etc.; see above siman 554:",
  "peri-megadim/2:_":
    "And if — Taz: permission is bodily even without danger, only monetary loss — permitted; siman 554 Magen Avraham; question on chol hamoed: great loss permitted; one may say there it is different; here any monetary loss if the wall falls and deteriorates more — permitted afterward to build of joy too; requires study:",
  "peri-megadim/4:_":
    "And even — Taz brought R' Shlomo; Bach too; wording doubtful; nevertheless implies for mourning itself for Shabbat honor they spread a laundered tablecloth (linen tablecloth, silk tablecloth) within zayin even though undershirt forbidden to wear — see Yoreh De'ah 489 Taz 1; here for Shabbat honor undershirt permitted, all the more hand and table cloths; but sheets perhaps they practice prohibition; requires study why stricter on undershirt for Shabbat, etc.; one may say on erev Shabbat; what Rabbenu Yerucham wrote seif 1 Shabbat Chazon wear only undershirt; here gloss implies for Shabbat honor wear whites like other Shabboses — implies all garments; must say custom is only undershirt; explained in Magen Avraham:",
  "peri-megadim/5:_":
    "And linen — Taz, Bach, Darkei Moshe; I had no time to examine properly; woman wearing whites — Rama gloss; Yoreh De'ah 489:2 implies there if she has a clean garment she wears it, not white; if only white — perhaps permitted there and here on Tisha B'Av itself for any mitzvah need; Peri Megadim letter 13; explained there:",
  "peri-megadim/8:_":
    "That — Taz; Magen Avraham 426:6; Or Sameach here 46: they practice to sanctify on motza'ei Tisha B'Av because the anointed one was born motza'ei Tisha B'Av — Ari z\"l; I too saw such practice; if possible taste before — fine; it appears if still walking barefoot and finished shoes, do not sanctify the moon:",
  "peri-megadim/9:_":
    "And permitted — Taz Yoreh De'ah 216:11 (Tosafot 216:1); here permitted even though wine was on 17 Tammuz and later became vinegar — still permitted; what he wrote prohibition became permission — seemingly siman 217 where not placed, etc.; Magen Avraham there: why is wine forbidden in vinegar? Therefore lentil and became honey, musk, milk changed category and taste unlike this still called wine from vinegar; explained letter 11:",
  "peri-megadim/13:_":
    "Haircut — Taz; Magen Avraham letter 11 permits for mitzvah need — see there; Mechaber regarding beard: whatever obstructs eating permitted — Taz here and Yoreh De'ah 390 Shach letter 1; haircut — man and woman forbidden; Yoreh De'ah 390 and Shach; woman after seven — Bach permits shaving temples; perhaps so here; sandek or mohel forbidden to shave in week Tisha B'Av falls — by Gemara law forbidden; do not be lenient for milah except their custom from Rosh Chodesh, all the more from 17 Tammuz for those who practice or the other's week for those who practice; siman 493 counting Magen Avraham letter 4; Or Sameach here letter 28; Yoreh De'ah 393: mourner may fix nails and shave to shave them — meaning nails; Levush fix nails and cut them; certainly head shaving not needed for milah only honor of milah; likewise mourner within thirty if another mohel exists — Ateret Zekenim forbids fixing his nails, for by law forbidden; brought what is written; Yoreh De'ah 386:15 all custom permits — by law forbidden; perhaps only not for pleasure and beauty; nail cutting for milah need — perhaps only within seven forbidden if another mohel, not because of nails but some joy for him — requires study:",
  "biur-halacha/2:א":
    "From Rosh Chodesh until the fast, etc. — one may be stringent on 17 Tammuz and 10 Tevet like Rosh Chodesh until fast [Ateret Zekenim, Pri Megadim]:",
  "biur-halacha/2:ב":
    "We reduce commerce, etc. — see Mishna Berurah: for many poskim intent is engage only for livelihood; Pri Megadim wrote: Israel whose livelihood is musical instruments for gentiles in banquet hall — see as livelihood permitted — see there; Darkei Chayim implies Pri Megadim permits only from 17 Tammuz until Rosh Chodesh:",
  "biur-halacha/2:ג":
    "Or building of molding and basin — even if he wishes to leave a cubit by a cubit, otherwise forever forbidden per Tur view as below siman 505; Rambam holds even leaving a cubit by a cubit forever forbidden to build molding and basin building as explained there in Beit Yosef — here must be from Rosh Chodesh Av and below forbidden even if he wishes to plaster beneath with clay plaster first [Machatzit HaShekel siman 505]:",
  "biur-halacha/3:א":
    "And linen garments have no giyutz concern — Tur's wording: because we are not concerned for their giyutz as they are close to the body; Ramban wrote the wording: because they are not giyutz and sweat and filth do not emerge from them — meaning linen garments close to the body:",
  "biur-halacha/16:א": "And for mitzvah need — permitted even in hot water:",
  "biur-halacha/16:ג":
    "Scrubbing the head in hot water — even if erev Shabbat Chazon falls on 9 Av; nevertheless l'chatchila beware before chatzot; b'dieved also after chatzot permitted [Pri Megadim]; further wrote regarding chatzot: on erev Shabbat after chatzot they do not make chatzot; likewise Rosh Chodesh Av no chatzot after midday:",
  "chatam-sofer/3:_":
    "Further there — NB see inside Ateret Zekenim who wrote Shabbat when Tisha B'Av falls and is postponed is not called a week Tisha B'Av falls within — see there [from the Gaon KS]:",
  "dagul-merevavah/2:_":
    "And to comb with a comb — forbidden in such remnants; I wonder Yoreh De'ah 390 permits even within seven mourning days, and Tisha B'Av is lighter than mourning; strained to say this fears public display; see Shach s.k. 3 in name of Hagahot Semak there permits specifically woman not man:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/1:_":
    "(1) [Seif 1] When Av enters we reduce joy — from Rosh Chodesh until 10th included, as Nachmad Lemareh vol. 2 Darkei Zera letter 71 — see there; one careful these ten days not to rejoice will merit the ten things the Holy One will renew in the future as Midrash Rabbah Bereishit section 15 — Yafeh Pa'aneach vol. 2 letter 1 — see there:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/7:_":
    "(7) There in gloss — baal brit, his mother; Chayei Adam there; Kitzur Shulchan Aruch there; Mishna Berurah 4; father's father of the son and mother's father of the birthing woman also practice leniency and wear Shabbat garments — they and their wives; similarly other relatives who would ordinarily wear Shabbat garments; Or Sameach there; however wrote for other relatives one should be stringent — see there; it appears all written for milah applies to pidyon haben; so below gloss seif 9 equates milah to pidyon — see there:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/8:_":
    "(8) There in gloss — wear Shabbat garments; although Shulchan Aruch below 559:8 rules baal brit wears other garments on Tisha B'Av itself as relative to Yom Tov, all the more Rosh Chodesh until Tisha B'Av — here teaches even actual whites not forbidden except on Tisha B'av day; further teaches even for Ashkenaz custom stringent on Shabbat Chazon not to change except undershirt — one might think milah Yom Tov overrides Shabbat and he should not change weekday clothes per custom; teaches they practice leniency for milah; Mateh Yehuda; Beit David Machberet 5 wrote unlike Or Zarua 76 who wrote the opposite in name of the Rav — see there:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/10:_":
    "(10) There in gloss — Shabbat garments; regarding haircut for baal brit — needless to say on Tisha B'Av itself forbidden; even from Rosh Chodesh until fast forbidden — Beit Yehudah vol. 2:85; so in his book Mateh Yehuda on this siman; Or Sameach letter 28; except wrote before the week Tisha B'Av falls perhaps permitted; so Mishnat Hasidim letter 13; Zera Emet there; Beit David Machberet 2; Novominsker Mahakam siman 28; Rav Yaavetz responsum vol. 3:2–3 ruled to forbid — see there; responsum Chatam Sofer 258 challenged Novominsker and concluded full permission even in week Tisha B'Av falls — wrote thus is public consensus — see there; responsum Binyan Tzion 31 wrote when Tisha B'Av is Shabbat certainly rely to shave even erev Shabbat — see there; responsum Or Ne'elam 10: Tisha B'Av Sunday, milah Thursday before — no week law, no Gemara prohibition — permit mohel and baal brit haircut for milah honor — see there; Beit David Shaarei Teshuvah 1; Kitzur Shulchan Aruch 122:15: before Shabbat Chazon permit, afterward forbid; it appears since most authorities forbid in week Tisha B'Av falls, be stringent since Gemara law; from 17 Tammuz and Tisha B'Av on Shabbat or Sunday — no week law per Shulchan Aruch below seif 4 — lenient if needed; all for those stringent haircut from 17 Tammuz or Rosh Chodesh; for Shulchan Aruch practice forbidden only in week Tisha B'Av falls — otherwise permitted if not that week:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/14:_":
    "(14) Custom of Israel not to say on Shabbat Chazon any kinah as in siddurim; so Egypt and large cities in Turkey; even Tisha B'Av on Shabbat they place on the table meat stew aphtora to honor Shabbat — who set a mouth to weep and mourn? Also public acceptance on holy Shabbat day — certainly fit to abolish this custom; Birkei Yosef 2; responsum Zaken Aharon 38 expanded to forbid — see there; responsum Radbaz vol. 3:245; Beit David Machberet 6 Shaarei Teshuvah letter 6:",
  "levushei-serad/3:_":
    "(Taz s.k. 1) as first reasoning — Tur brought disputants and holds all building and planting forbidden:",
  "levushei-serad/9:_":
    "(s.k. 12) it appears. Rashi z\"l Taanit 29 wrote this reason they forbid laundering to leave after Tisha B'Av — diverts mind from mourning and engages in garments; even giving to gentile launderers forbidden; implied in Rama seif 7 forbidden to give garments to launder even if told launder after Tisha B'Av as Magen Avraham s.k. 9 — must be diversion from mourning and giving garments to launder:",
  "levushei-serad/12:_":
    "(Magen Avraham s.k. 18) know — Rav Sheshet's language Taanit 29 to prove not like R' Nachman who permits launder and leave — if so why were all launderers in Rav's house idle; therefore Magen Avraham used this language to prove even traveler forbidden to launder:",
  "levushei-serad/16:_":
    "(s.k. 23) Rabbenu Yonah's view to be lenient — seif 6 only forbids wearing on Shabbat when Tisha B'Av falls therein; therefore Mechaber wrote here; one should be stringent:",
  "netiv-chayim/2:_":
    "(Magen Avraham s.k. 36) for adults forbidden — NB as he mentioned this reasoning in Tur:",
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger/7:_":
    "(Magen Avraham s.k. 42) and on Shabbat permitted — responsum Beit Yehudah end of book customs letter 61: I heard from my teacher that Rosh Chodesh law is like Shabbat:",
  "yad-ephraim/7:_":
    "There — forbidden in all building, therefore he too. Requires study: our Talmud does not argue; as Yerushalmi permits joy building if leaning to fall and monetary loss — likewise for us, only without loss forbidden in all building; if leaning to fall permitted. It appears our Talmud implies forbidding all building — Yerushalmi does not argue except building for profit called joy as Beit Yosef in name of Ran — see there; one view holds since only joy building forbidden, if loss even joy building permitted since not for joy but for loss; unlike view forbidding even if not for joy — no permission even for loss without danger:",
  "yad-ephraim/14:_":
    "(s.k. 15) even if he told him to launder, etc. — Or Sameach lenient if told launder after Tisha B'Av; perhaps Magen Avraham meant even if impossible to launder after Tisha B'Av — missing one letter? should read even if impossible to launder — abbreviation as written; like below s.k. 18 disputing Bach on traveler to distant places:",
  "yad-ephraim/17:_":
    "(Taz end s.k. 5) the wording \"sleeping,\" etc. — his words seem strange; what he wrote \"therefore linen garments\" refers if laundered — forbidden to giyutz; per his words initially forbidden to giyutz in their giyutz, then wrote our giyutz too forbidden, etc. — strange Ramban did not mention initially to decide forbidden in such linen later called \"therefore\"; further he wrote afterward \"nevertheless our laundering permitted and all linen everywhere even in their giyutz permitted,\" etc.; if so what means \"nevertheless our laundering\" — wool garments, then only our laundering permitted and linen everywhere, meaning even their laundering; initially not dealing with linen at all; also \"all linen\" between whites and dyed implies initially not linen; main explanation per my humble opinion as Bach wrote: wool garments; \"and likewise pre-laundered,\" etc. — not only forbidden to wear pre-laundered, forbidden even to launder to leave in their laundering in wool garments; although afterward wrote law of wearing forbidden even alone — coming to renew even linen forbidden; initially wool law is simple; Bach on Ramban's words: their laundering stricter than our giyutz and their giyutz regarding pre-laundered even not from the press — strange to me, for gezeirah on earth, etc., implies their giyutz stricter; further they explicitly said our giyutz like their laundering; if you say \"from the press\" applies only to giyutz — he should explain whence and clarify proof; rather Ramban's words imply also for laundering, as Torat HaAdam 61b regarding mourner and laundered in my view forbidden, etc.; likewise pre-laundered from press — applies to laundering; therefore Ramban: all pre-laundered forbidden even to leave after Shabbat; our giyutz too forbidden in new and pre-laundered from press, etc.; \"from press\" even if left after Shabbat applies; if not afraid I would say possible typo (new between pre-laundered); therefore correct emendation pre-laundered with shin meaning from press; law does not imply so; therefore as stated:",
  "baer-heitev/10:ב":
    "(2) For an infant. And likewise a cup — after the milah it is customary to give to the infant; specifically an infant who does not know to mourn for Jerusalem (Magen Avraham; implied there not specifically mitzvah need but any such Shabbat permitted since he does not know to mourn; Eliyah Rabbah disagrees — only mitzvah need — see there):",
  "beur-hagra/6:א":
    "(66) New garments, etc. — from their dispute on giyutz implied in laundering even Ra'avad concedes no difference white and dyed; we say there: this teaches mourner forbidden in laundering — even water alone without giyutz; similarly new garments per Tanna Kamma and Rabbi Yehuda is giyutz even on old; Yerushalmi: what is giyutz? new wool and whitened linen — linen specifically new white, wool new even dyed; we do not rule like Yerushalmi here only other types forbidden only white, for we rule like R' Eliezer per R' Shimon that Rava holds like him; nevertheless learn from Yerushalmi new white garments no difference linen and other types; we say: with what do they rejoice in Israel? whitened linen garments — behold giyutz in them; what is written linen has no giyutz — in old because sweat does not leave; all distinctions there in old; in new all forbidden; see above seif 63 even linen and our laundering forbidden by law:",
  "beur-hagra/9:א":
    "(69) There are those accustomed, etc. — some add, etc. Shiyurei Korban in that Yerushalmi: not wine not meat — see Halakhot Gedolot, so Yalkut 553; depends dispute above seven days; first reading primary as Gaon wrote, therefore \"these women\" — their craft as Ketubot, whole Yerushalmi sugya is melacha — see there; essence of reason as chapter 3 Bava Batra: R' Yehoshua ben Elisha taught from Temple destruction fit to decree, etc., but we do not decree, etc.; they practiced these days when one can stand briefly; Mishnah forbade break meal of Tisha B'Av and they added:",
  "chatam-sofer/2:_":
    "There in gloss — Shabbat garments. NB however forbidden haircut in week Tisha B'Av falls; similarly Rama — see s.k. 28:",
  "chatam-sofer/5:_":
    "There Mechaber seif 2 — see Dagul Merevavah. NB it appears: on Shabbat when wife goes to synagogue preferable for father before son's wedding, for husband obligated unlike father; although groom precedes husband's wife in obligation — Magen Avraham 282; husband not comparable to groom before wedding when they sing for him; nevertheless father of groom not comparable — clear above. [Gaon end quote]:",
  "chatam-sofer/6:_":
    "There Magen Avraham s.k. 14 — not accustomed haircut every week. NB mourning not evident if haircuts every Shabbat, otherwise only erev Shabbat as above — my humble opinion. Nevertheless growing beard in our time, great disgrace, judge like compelled by mourning — below seif 15; if combines some leniency such as bringing infant per Magen Avraham s.k. 3, room to lenient somewhat from abundance of hair:",
  "chatam-sofer/8:_":
    "There Mechaber seif 8 not to drink. NB see Tur and Beit Yosef 513 Yerushalmi not wine not meat — below seif 69; requires study from end chazakat habatim fruit not eaten water not drunk — say there if so would not drink — separatists cannot exist forever without water; meat too — separatists without meat; nevertheless impossible for most of community; since impossible for most, decree does not apply unlike our custom short time Rosh Chodesh until fast — therefore meat and wine most can manage without they forbade; water and fruit most cannot manage even briefly — not practiced; strained:",
  "chatam-sofer/9:_":
    "There Mechaber seif 16. NB one compelled thirty days mourning for parents — my father Rav z\"l responsum 158 like Noda B'Yehuda Moed Katan Orach Chayim 14: for elder measure of rebuke thirty days great disgrace; nevertheless wrote not lenient on Bach stringency Taz s.k. 14; sufficient lenient in shaving when insufficient days they will rebuke [Gaon end quote]:",
  "chatam-sofer/10:_":
    "There Magen Avraham s.k. 42; in my humble opinion one may say per Taz to bless Shehecheyanu daily between straits lest die before mitzvah; one may say previously they permitted weekdays from this reason, permitted Shabbat at least; again such short time until Shabbat no death concern, therefore weekdays should not bless, Shabbat must permit; responsum Chacham Tzvi 116 distinguish short and long; Ya'arot Devash third derashah part 2 until Shabbat short — see there; since Shabbat more reason to bless than weekday, permitted Shabbat forbade weekday — well since until Shabbat no death concern he will bless then as above correct; Magen Avraham 568 s.k. 13, Machatzit HaShekel. [Gaon responsum Orach Chayim 103]:",
  "chatam-sofer/11:_":
    "There Magen Avraham in that s.k. mourner forbidden to bless. Taz 471 s.k. 8 in Maggid Mishneh appears mourner should not bless Shehecheyanu — requires study, for there if no Shehecheyanu loses blessing; likewise between straits he blesses as here; perhaps Maggid Mishneh meant only public Shehecheyanu — requires study [Gaon end quote]:",
  "kaf-hachayyim/13:_":
    "(13) There gloss — only undershirt; permitted even if Tisha B'Av on Shabbat (Levush 16). Stringent not to change it is foolish custom, not garment but sweat (Rashal 27; Mateh Moshe 376; Magen Avraham s.k. 4). Or Zarua: those accustomed not to change undershirt have support — see there. All per our Rav z\"l; Maran in Beit Yosef end 552 after Gema and Mordechai wrote they did not practice; Radbaz responsum vol. 2:793 protested, Ashkenaz not changing on Shabbat — all Galilee did not practice and did well, against law — expanded see there; Gaon Mahar\"r Tzvi though Ashkenazi practiced changing nothing from other Shabboses even Tisha B'Av on Shabbat — son Rav Yaavetz siddur p. 60 — see there; Bach Mechaber or 4; Chayei Adam kelal 133 end 21 Vilna practice per Gra Shabbat garments change another garment — end quote; Or Chaim on Chayei Adam many communities wear all Shabbat garments change nothing even Shabbat when Tisha B'Av falls — see there; obvious \"many communities\" means Ashkenaz; Sephardic all practice not changing this Shabbat as Beit Yosef and Radbaz; Shulchan Gavoah: not white or important like festival garments; Yafeh Lalev vol. 3:2 custom not whites like summer Shabboses but dyed like winter — end quote; this per their custom; some change nothing wear whites too, only not festival garments; below 552:10:",
  "mishnah-berurah/7:ז":
    "(54) However if known, etc. — by law craftsman for gentile certainly not happy from this, forbidden only marit ayin they think it belongs to Jew [therefore first said stam forbidden for others, even gentiles]; therefore if known publicized, etc., permitted even accept work after Rosh Chodesh and do it:",
  "shaarei-teshuvah/3:_":
    "And baal brit. See Ba'er Heitev; Pri Megadim part 3 siman 37 challenged Ba'er Heitev — should not infer he did not write permission to shave — examine other side he did not write prohibition — may say relied on permitting whites, likewise shave since customary shave for it is his Yom Tov; Machatzit HaShekel in Zera Emet forbids haircut and whites in week word falls; challenged Pri Megadim Rama permitted only Shabbat garments not whites; Bnei Yehuda letter 5 forbid haircut permit whites — see Maharam Benet; responsum Or Ne'elam 10 Tisha B'Av Sunday milah Thursday before — no week law, no Gemara prohibition — room permit mohel and baal brit shave for milah honor; Pri Megadim challenged Ba'er Heitev understood Yoreh De'ah 390 shaving is head — nail cutting; Darkei Moshe did not mention shaving; Levush, etc.; vain challenge, Ba'er Heitev concluded with it see Beer HaGolah Bach there Darkei Moshe did not mention shaving — perhaps nails; Ba'er Heitev brought Levush Bach challenged should say \"and shave them,\" etc. — see there (Or Zarua, Or Zuta copied except omitted 390 refers nails because showed place for Noda B'Yehuda; Or Zarua Zuta before week room permit haircut too; if Shabbat postponed poskim ruled no week law — see there) (Pri Megadim advocated custom mourners shushbinin bring to chuppah and eat, Taz forbids eat there, lenient have whom to rely — Rosh HaCholeitz, etc.; other mourners practice prohibition, shushbinin great mitzvah, therefore lenient — see there; our country stringent per Taz only relatives serving inside meal like hosts eating afterward; improper musical singing heard, all the more mourner parent or wife serving — refrain singers) — Yoreh De'ah end 391 nail cutting for immersion on Tisha B'Av, Shach, Noda B'Yehuda, Magen Avraham bringing in taking out infant not included — all the more honorees passing infant; those on Elijah's chair, sandek's knees, holding at \"asher kidshanu,\" etc.; somewhat lenient seat on sandek's knees per custom sandek's feet like altar (Yoreh De'ah 265 like incense offering — Noda B'Yehuda 26: resolve Maharil not give sandek two sons lest grievance against sandek not mohel — if sandek's feet like altar offering incense, mohel like incense, etc. — see there; difficult sandek is altar — how from Torah not change, we find only offerer from \"they shall place incense,\" etc.; support Yoma 43: R' Yehuda no lottery for coal pan — uncommon, separate lottery, etc. — if so coal pan for incense also by lottery like offerer) — not seen practiced; Prague custom father places infant on sandek's feet — fine; father's father and mother's father lenient Shabbat garments — they and wives; recall lenient other relatives who would wear Shabbat garments — requires study; pain for father and mother when close relatives do not share joy by whites; other relatives stringent; Machatzit HaShekel Zera Emet baal brit forbidden new garments this week; women kvater to synagogue passing infant to men — wear Shabbat garments since only milah mitzvah; Pri Megadim:",
  "turei-zahav/10:_":
    "From Rosh Chodesh until fast. Bava Batra of Taanit they disagree — R' Meir and Rav Shmuel bar Gadyon: R' Meir prohibition until fast; Rav Shmuel bar Gadyon entire week; Rava ruled like R' Meir not forbidden after fast even that week; Rashal responsum 54: some practice no meat three weeks until Shabbat Nachamu as Minhagim — not well since meat-wine reasons passed; concluded: even stringent until Shabbat like Rav Shmuel bar Gadyon — improper conduct, Berachot chapter 1: R' Tarfon traveling inclined read per Beit Shammai, endangered by bandits; told: you should have obligated yourself since transgressed Beit Hillel; Tosafot: even though stringent like Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel permits incline and read — nevertheless since intended transgress Beit Hillel, even stringent liable death; same here halacha like R' Meir, he stringent like Rav Shmuel bar Gadyon — called sinner afflicting himself — end quote; Rav's words unclear, Tosafot end name R' Shim'ei: regarding Keriat Shema — even b'dieved fulfilled per Beit Hillel, not correct as Mishnah — should have obligated; if acted Beit Shammai for Beit Hillel did nothing — end quote; if so R' Tarfon deserved punishment only because Beit Shammai action incorrect for Beit Hillel; unlike here R' Meir vs Rav Shmuel — he did nothing against R' Meir acting like Rav Shmuel; Maharachatz disagrees for another reason — end quote; certainly stringent here has no sin, only should not say fears Rav Shmuel's view — Heaven forbid disagreeing Rava — rather be stringent beyond letter silently:",
  "yad-ephraim/5:_":
    "There in name of Rashal — see Yesh Shtar Yevamot chapter 4 siman 37:",
};
